$ has one local minimum at\n\n$$\nx = -\\sqrt 2\n$$\n\nand one local maximum at\n\n$$\nx = \\sqrt 2\n$$" 13194139534521 false] [17592186105490 1145 "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 6-3x^2.\n$$\n\nSolving $f'(x) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n3(2-x^2)=0 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad x=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=\\pm \\sqrt 2.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x = -2, 0, 2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-2) & =6-3(-2)^{2}=-6 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =6-3(0)^{2}=6 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =6-3(2)^{2}=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $\\left(-\\infty,-\\sqrt{2}\\right)$ | $-\\sqrt{2}$ | $\\left(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}\\right)$ | $\\sqrt{2}$ | $\\left(\\sqrt{2},\\infty\\right)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-2$ |  | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| $f'$ | $\\color{blue}-$ | $0$ | $\\color{red}+$ | $0$ | $\\color{blue}-$ |\n| $f$ | $\\color{blue}\\searrow$ | min | $\\color{red}\\nearrow$ | max | $\\color{blue}\\searrow$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-\\sqrt{2},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=-\\sqrt{2}$ is a local minimum.\n\n- At $x=\\sqrt{2},$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=\\sqrt{2}$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has one local minimum at $x = -\\sqrt 2$ and one local maximum at $x = \\sqrt 2.$" 13194139534521 true] [17592186105492 1145 "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\nf'(x) \t=3x^2,\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n3x^2 = 0 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad x=0.\n$$\n\nSo $x = 0$ is the only stationary point of $f(x).$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x = -1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =3(-1)^{2}=3>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =3(1)^{2}=3>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $\\left(-\\infty, 0\\right)$ | $0$ | $\\left(0,\\infty\\right)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| $f'$ | $\\color{red}+$ | $0$ | $\\color{red}+$ |\n| $f$ | $\\color{red}\\nearrow$ |  | $\\color{red}\\nearrow$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=0.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=0$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nSo, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema." 13194139534521 true] [17592186105492 1145 "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\nf'(x) =3x^2\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n3x^2 = 0 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad x=0\n$$\n\nSo $x = 0$ is the only stationary point of $f(x)$. Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x = -1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(-1) &= 3(-1)^{2} = 3 > 0 \\\\\nf^{′}(1) &= 3(1)^{2} = 3 > 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $\\left(-\\infty, 0\\right)$ | $0$ | $\\left(0,\\infty\\right)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| $f'$ | $\\color{red}+$ | $0$ | $\\color{red}+$ |\n| $f$ | $\\color{red}\\nearrow$ |  | $\\color{red}\\nearrow$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=0$. Therefore, we conclude that $x=0$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nSo, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema." 13194139534521 false] [17592186105493 1100 "Find the relative maximum and minimum points of the function $f(x) = (2x+3)e^x$." 13194139534521 false] [17592186105493 1100 "Find the relative maximum and minimum points of the function $f(x) = (2x+3)e^x.$" 13194139534521 true] [17592186105493 1145 "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= (2x + 3)e^{x} + 2e^{x} = (2x + 5)e^{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving $f'(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2x + 5)e^{x} &= 0 \\\\\n2x + 5 &= 0 \\\\\nx &= -\\frac{5}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo\n\n$$\nx = -\\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nis a stationary point. Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x = -5,0$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(-5) &= (2(-5) + 5)e^{-5} =-\\frac{5}{e^{5}} < 0 \\\\\nf^{′}(0) &= (2(0) + 5)e^{0} = 5 > 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $\\left(-\\infty, -\\dfrac{5}{2}\\right)$ | $-\\dfrac{5}{2}$ | $\\left(-\\dfrac{5}{2},\\infty\\right)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Test Point | $-5$ |  | $0$ |\n| $f'$ | $\\color{blue}-$ | $0$ | $\\color{red}+$ |\n| $f$ | $\\color{blue}\\searrow$ | min | $\\color{red}\\nearrow$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\nAt\n\n$$\nx=-\\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nthe first derivative changes sign from negative to positive.\n\nSo, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a local minimum at\n\n$$\nx = -\\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$" 13194139534521 false] [17592186105493 1145 "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(2x+3){e}^{x}+2{e}^{x}=(2x+5){e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving $f'(x)=0$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x+5){e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ 2x+5 & =0 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x = -\\dfrac{5}{2}$ is a stationary point. Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x = -5,0$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-5) & =(2(-5)+5){e}^{-5}=-\\frac{5}{{e}^{5}}<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =(2(0)+5){e}^{0}=5>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $\\left(-\\infty, -\\dfrac{5}{2}\\right)$ | $-\\dfrac{5}{2}$ | $\\left( -\\dfrac{5}{2},\\infty\\right)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-5$ |  | $0$ |\n| $f'$ | $\\color{blue}-$ | $0$ | $\\color{red}+$ |\n| $f$ | $\\color{blue}\\searrow$ | min | $\\color{red}\\nearrow$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\nAt $x=-\\dfrac{5}{2},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive.\n\nSo, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a local minimum at $x = -\\dfrac{5}{2}.$" 13194139534521 true] [17592186105633 1020 #edb/ref 17592186292964 13194139534521 true] [17592186105633 1020 #edb/ref 17592186292971 13194139534521 true] [17592186105633 1020 #edb/ref 17592186292999 13194139534521 true] [17592186105633 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293002 13194139534521 true] [17592186105633 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293005 13194139534521 true] [17592186105634 1020 #edb/ref 17592186292978 13194139534521 true] [17592186105634 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293015 13194139534521 true] [17592186105634 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293022 13194139534521 true] [17592186105634 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293029 13194139534521 true] [17592186105635 1020 #edb/ref 17592186292985 13194139534521 true] [17592186105635 1020 #edb/ref 17592186292992 13194139534521 true] [17592186105635 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293008 13194139534521 true] [17592186106129 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534521 true] [17592186106129 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nFor the function $f(x) = x - 2\\sin x$ defined for $x ∈ (0, 2π)$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $x = \\frac{π}{3}$ is a local minimum\n2. $x = \\frac{π}{3}$ is a local maximum\n3. $x = \\frac{5π}{3}$ is a local maximum" 13194139534521 false] [17592186106129 1100 "For the function $f(x)=x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ defined for $x\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is a local minimum\n- $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is a local maximum\n- $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ is a local maximum" 13194139534521 true] [17592186106129 1145 "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$   on $(0,2\\pi )$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ are stationary points. Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points  $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\pi ,\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=1-\\sqrt{3}<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\pi ) & =1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi =3>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}) & =1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})=1-\\sqrt{3}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ | $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3})$ | $\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ | $(\\frac{5\\pi }{3},2\\pi )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ |  | $\\pi $ |  | $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is a local minimum.\n\n- At $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3},$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ is a local maximum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534521 true] [17592186106291 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534521 true] [17592186106291 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nFor which value of $x$ does the function $f(x) = x^{2}e^{x}$ have a local minimum?" 13194139534521 false] [17592186106291 1100 "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)={x}^{2}{e}^{x}$ have a local minimum?" 13194139534521 true] [17592186106291 1145 "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{x}+2x{e}^{x}=(x+2)x{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)x{e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ (x+2)x & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=-2$ and $x=0$ are stationary points. Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-3,-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-3) & =(-3+2)(-3){e}^{-3}=3{e}^{-3}>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =(-1+2)(-1){e}^{-1}=-{e}^{-1}<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =(1+2)(1){e}^{1}=3e>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $-2$ | $(-2,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-3$ |  | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-2,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=-2$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=0$ is a local minimum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local minimum at $x=0.$" 13194139534521 true] [17592186106292 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534521 true] [17592186106292 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nGiven the function $f(x) =-x^{5}$, which of the following statements are true?" 13194139534521 false] [17592186106292 1100 "Given the function $f(x)=-{x}^{5},$ which of the following statements are true?" 13194139534521 true] [17592186106292 1145 "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-5{x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-5{x}^{4}=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nSo $x=0$ is the only stationary point of $f(x).$ Next, to determine whether this point is local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =-5(-1)^{4}=-5<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =-5(1)^{4}=-5<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ |  | $↘$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=0.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=0$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nHence, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema." 13194139534521 true] [17592186292964 1017 #edb/ref 17592186292965 13194139534521 true] [17592186292964 1089 "q-113750" 13194139534521 true] [17592186292964 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534521 true] [17592186292964 1100 "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=12x+9{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3}$ have a local maximum?" 13194139534521 true] [17592186292964 1113 #uuid "b49a83d0-be9c-5ea2-af93-457dbf50bb25" 13194139534521 true] [17592186292964 1145 "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =12+18x-12{x}^{2} \\\\  & =-6(2{x}^{2}-3x-2) \\\\  & =-6(x-2)(2x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-6(x-2)(2x+1)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{1}{2},2.\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=-\\f